<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For lockers, <a href="https://www.thisoldhouse.com/entryways-mudrooms/21018707/9-smart-mudroom-design-ideas" target="_blank" rel="noopener">This Old House suggests</a> making them as large as 2 feet wide if you want room for backpacks and bulky winter jackets to hang side by side. The same article shows how even a 4-by-5-foot back hall can become a mudroom with the right built-ins, so a small space is not a deal breaker.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Quick Pick: Mudroom Ideas by Situation</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Your situation</th><th>Start with these ideas</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Busy family, lots of gear</td><td>Lockers per person (#1), baskets up top (#3), kid-height hooks (#4)</td></tr><tr><td>Shoes everywhere</td><td>Bench with shoe shelves (#5), boot tray (#6), shoe limit (#7)</td></tr><tr><td>No real mudroom</td><td>Entryway mudroom wall (#9), slim bench (#10), hooks in a row (#11)</td></tr><tr><td>Mudroom in the laundry room</td><td>Split the room (#13), counter over machines (#14), utility sink (#15)</td></tr><tr><td>Keys, mail and leashes lost</td><td>Peg rail (#16), key and mail station (#17)</td></tr><tr><td>Messy floors</td><td>Washable runner (#18), boot tray (#6)</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How to Set Up a Mudroom in 6 Steps</h2>



<ol class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>List what comes through the door.</strong> Shoes, coats, bags, sports gear, pet supplies, mail and keys.</li>



<li><strong>Measure the wall and the walkway.</strong> Leave a clear path to the door and room for the door to swing.</li>



<li><strong>Choose one spot per person.</strong> A locker, a hook and a basket each makes it clear who owns what.</li>



<li><strong>Add a place to sit.</strong> A bench makes taking off boots easier and hides shoes underneath.</li>



<li><strong>Go vertical.</strong> Hooks at two heights, a shelf with baskets and a peg rail use the wall, not the floor.</li>



<li><strong>Protect the floor.</strong> A boot tray and a washable rug catch water, mud and snow.</li>
</ol>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">18 Mudroom Ideas That Work</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">These mudroom ideas are grouped by what they solve, from full built-ins to small entryway setups.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Mudroom Lockers and Cubbies</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/mudroom-02-lockers.jpg" alt="Mudroom lockers: four open white oak lockers with hooks for coats and backpacks, baskets on top and shoe cubbies below the bench" class="wp-image-1013" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/mudroom-02-lockers.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/mudroom-02-lockers-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/mudroom-02-lockers-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/mudroom-02-lockers-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">1. Give each person a locker</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Open lockers with a hook, a shelf and a cubby below give everyone their own zone. Kids know exactly where their backpack goes, and nobody&#8217;s coat ends up under someone else&#8217;s.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">2. Skip the doors</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Open lockers are faster to use than closed cabinets. If you want a calmer look, put doors only on the top section where off-season gear lives.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">3. Put baskets on the top shelf</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">One basket per person holds hats, gloves and scarves in winter and sunscreen and caps in summer. Swap the contents with the seasons.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">4. Add hooks at kid height</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A second row of hooks lower down lets kids hang their own coats and bags. It&#8217;s a small change that makes the whole mudroom work better.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Mudroom Benches and Shoe Storage</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/mudroom-03-shoe-bench.jpg" alt="Mudroom bench with slatted shoe shelves holding sneakers and boots, a linen cushion and a basket of hats and gloves" class="wp-image-1014" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/mudroom-03-shoe-bench.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/mudroom-03-shoe-bench-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/mudroom-03-shoe-bench-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/mudroom-03-shoe-bench-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">5. Choose a bench with shoe shelves</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A bench with slatted shelves below gives you a place to sit and a place for everyday shoes. Slats let wet shoes dry and are easy to wipe clean.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">6. Add a boot tray</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A tray under or next to the bench catches melting snow and mud before it reaches the floor. It&#8217;s one of the cheapest mudroom ideas with the biggest payoff.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">7. Set a shoe limit</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Keep one or two pairs per person in the mudroom and move the rest to closets. A shoe cubby for each person makes the limit easy to see.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">8. Use a lift-top storage bench</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A bench with a hinged seat hides sports gear, reusable bags or pet supplies. Choose one with a slow-close hinge so fingers stay safe.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Small Mudroom Ideas</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/mudroom-04-small-mudroom.jpg" alt="Small mudroom ideas: narrow hallway by the back door with a wall-mounted bench, a row of hooks, a slim shoe cabinet and a runner rug" class="wp-image-1012" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/mudroom-04-small-mudroom.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/mudroom-04-small-mudroom-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/mudroom-04-small-mudroom-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/mudroom-04-small-mudroom-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">9. Turn an entryway wall into a mudroom</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">No separate room? A single wall by the back door is enough. Entryway mudroom ideas usually come down to three pieces: hooks, a bench and a spot for shoes. Our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/small-entryway-ideas/">small entryway ideas</a> show more ways to fit storage into a tight entry.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">10. Mount a slim bench on the wall</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A wall-mounted bench leaves the floor open underneath for shoes or baskets, and it feels less bulky in a narrow hall.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">11. Line up hooks in a single row</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A long row of hooks with a shelf above holds more than a freestanding coat rack and takes up almost no floor space.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">12. Use a narrow shoe cabinet</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Tilt-out shoe cabinets are only a few inches deep and hide a surprising number of pairs. They&#8217;re a smart fit for small mudrooms and hallways.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Laundry Mudroom Ideas</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/mudroom-05-laundry-mudroom.jpg" alt="Laundry mudroom combo with a washer and dryer under a wood counter on one side and a bench with hooks and cubbies on the other" class="wp-image-1010" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/mudroom-05-laundry-mudroom.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/mudroom-05-laundry-mudroom-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/mudroom-05-laundry-mudroom-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/mudroom-05-laundry-mudroom-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">13. Split the room into two zones</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Put the washer and dryer on one wall and the bench and hooks on the other. Each zone has its own job, and dirty clothes go straight from the door to the machine.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">14. Add a counter over front-loaders</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A counter across the top of front-loading machines gives you a place to fold and a landing spot for bags. Our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/how-to-organize-a-laundry-room/">guide to organizing a laundry room</a> shows how to plan the rest of the space.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">15. Add a utility sink</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A deep sink near the door is perfect for muddy boots, paws and sports gear, and it keeps the mess out of the kitchen.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Mudroom Wall Organization and Furniture</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/mudroom-06-hook-wall.jpg" alt="Mudroom hook wall with a peg rail holding hats, a dog leash and tote bags, a key and mail organizer and an umbrella stand" class="wp-image-1011" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/mudroom-06-hook-wall.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/mudroom-06-hook-wall-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/mudroom-06-hook-wall-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/mudroom-06-hook-wall-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">16. Hang a peg rail</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A wooden peg rail holds hats, leashes, tote bags and umbrellas. It works in any style and costs far less than built-in lockers.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">17. Create a key and mail station</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A small wall organizer with key hooks and a mail slot stops keys from going missing and keeps paper off the counter.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">18. Choose washable mudroom furniture and rugs</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">When you shop for mudroom furniture, look for sealed wood, metal or wipeable finishes, and pair them with a washable runner. A mudroom takes a lot of wear, so easy cleaning matters more than looks. If your back door is near the stairs, our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/under-stairs-storage-ideas/">under stairs storage ideas</a> show how to add extra cubbies there.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Common Mudroom Mistakes</h2>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Blocking the door swing.</strong> Keep the area in front of the door clear.</li>



<li><strong>Hooks that are too high for kids.</strong> Add a second row at their height.</li>



<li><strong>Too many shoes.</strong> Keep only the pairs you wear often by the door.</li>



<li><strong>Closed storage for everyday items.</strong> Open hooks and cubbies get used; doors get ignored.</li>



<li><strong>No plan for wet gear.</strong> Add a boot tray, a mat and a place for coats to dry.</li>
</ul>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/mudroom-07-before-after.jpg" alt="Mudroom before and after: shoes and coats piled by the back door next to the same corner organized with a bench, cubbies and hooks" class="wp-image-1009" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/mudroom-07-before-after.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/mudroom-07-before-after-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/mudroom-07-before-after-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/mudroom-07-before-after-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The before-and-after above is the same corner by the back door. A bench, cubbies and a row of hooks are all it took to get shoes and coats off the floor. That&#8217;s the goal of all good mudroom ideas.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Mudroom FAQ</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How do you make a mudroom in a small space?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Use one wall by the door: a slim bench, a row of hooks with a shelf above and a narrow shoe cabinet. Keep the walkway clear and use baskets for small items.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How high should mudroom hooks be?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Adult hooks are often placed around 60 to 66 inches from the floor, with a second row for kids around 40 to 48 inches. Adjust to the people in your home.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How deep should a mudroom bench be?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Most mudroom benches are about 16 to 18 inches deep and about 18 inches high, which is comfortable for sitting and leaves room for shoes underneath.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Can a laundry room be a mudroom?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Yes. Put the machines on one wall and a bench with hooks on the other, and add a counter over front-loaders for folding. A utility sink is a great bonus.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If shoes still pile up by the door, see our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/shoe-storage-ideas/">shoe storage ideas</a> for 20 ways to store them in the entryway, closet, under the stairs and under the bed.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Planning a mudroom that doubles as a laundry area? Check that your washer and dryer will fit first with our free <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/washer-dryer-space-calculator/">washer and dryer space calculator</a>.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If your mudroom also has a console or narrow table by the door, our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/entryway-table-decor/">entryway table decor guide</a> shows how to style it so it stays useful and stops collecting the daily drop pile.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Caps, beanies and sun hats pile up fast in a mudroom. Our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/hat-storage-ideas/">hat storage ideas</a> show how to give each type the right hook, basket or shelf.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In the UK this space is usually called a boot room; see our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/boot-room-ideas/">boot room ideas</a> for small British homes.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Final Thoughts</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The best mudroom ideas give every person and every item one spot by the door: a hook, a cubby, a place for shoes and a basket for the small stuff. Start with a bench and a row of hooks, measure your walkway, and build from there.</p>



